anv: keep push descriptor data in host memory, never read it back from write-combined memory

anv_push_descriptor_set_init() keeps the previous descriptors on a new push with the same layout by copying the old
descriptor memory into the new allocation (the "TODO: keep a copy of the old data on the host" there). That memory
comes from the surface/dynamic/indirect-push state pools, which on a discrete GPU are write-combined VRAM: every push
reads it back over PCIe uncached. Minecraft 26.3 (Vulkan) spent 17% of its render thread in that memmove (646 of
3872 samples at ~950 fps, anv_push_descriptor_set_init+563 / +815).

The command buffer's push sets now keep a host copy (push_set->host_shadow): descriptor writes go to host memory
(set->desc_{surface,sampler}_mem.map points at it), the copy keeps the previous descriptors without any read, and
anv_push_descriptor_set_upload() streams the whole set into the GPU allocation after each push's writes (write-only,
which write-combined memory does at full speed). The GPU addresses, sizes and the re-allocation rule
(set_used_on_gpu) are unchanged. Other users of anv_push_descriptor_set_init (ASTC emulation) keep the old path.

--- a/src/intel/vulkan/anv_descriptor_set.c
